@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@ The feedback must span argument generation and execution as one continuous activ
 
 The formal Tool Definition reads the preceding activity state when its `tool/call` start is assembled. It inherits the first-delta time as its presentation start, then removes the matching pending value in the same assembly flush. Pending calls are keyed by stream index and kept in insertion order; after the Assistant finalizes, their synthetic placement advances behind that Assistant and then behind each emitted formal call. Formal nodes keep their authoritative `tool/call` sequence, so parallel calls preserve product transcript order while each replaces exactly one pending row. The retained start facts survive step closure so replay and interruption do not reset elapsed time.
 
-`ui-tool` renders a pending value as the same non-expandable title and icon row used by ordinary Tools, with no argument summary or body. One wrapper-owned activity chrome follows both pending and formal rows: a gray elapsed timer and gray `ing` remain visible throughout argument generation and execution, and a Think-style scanning glare crosses the running row. Settlement freezes the timer and stops the glare. The status position is empty by default and reveals gray `Done` only while the finished row is hovered or keyboard-focused. Existing error and interrupted row semantics remain authoritative, and reduced-motion preferences remove the scanning glare.
+`ui-tool` renders a pending value as the same non-expandable title and icon row used by ordinary Tools, with no argument summary or body. One wrapper-owned activity chrome follows both pending and formal rows: a gray elapsed timer and gray `ing` sit inline immediately after the Tool title or summary throughout argument generation and execution, and a Think-style scanning glare crosses the running row. Settlement freezes the timer, formats positive subsecond work as `<1s`, and stops the glare. The status position is empty by default and reveals gray `Done` only while the finished row is hovered or keyboard-focused. Existing error and interrupted row semantics remain authoritative, and reduced-motion preferences remove the scanning glare.
 
